speechtext:
Yes. I say that the act
provides for inspection at the refineries. At one time it was necessary to prevent gasoline from being mixed in with coal oil for illuminating purposes, and, gasoline being a byproduct, that sometimes occurred. But since the advent of the internal combustion engine every effort has been made to secure gasoline for the purposes of automotive power, with the result that the refiners themselves are taking every possible means to see that the gasoline is produced for the purposes for which they require it, and very careful tests are being made by the refiners themselves. While we have our officers there they are simply duplicating the tests which are already being made in the interests of the refiners.
In addition to that, the provincial governments have very long lists of requirements. I might quote briefly from provisions contained in the Gasoline Handling Act of Ontario to give some indication of the supervision. particularly that which is exercised by that province. I will not read the items in full, but under the provisions of section 12 of that act:
2680	COMMONS
Petroleum and Naphtha Inspection Act
The lieutenant-governor in council may make regulations,-
(a)	providing for the appointment of . . . inspectors, . . .
(b)	providing for the issuing of licences. . . *
(c)	prescribing the records and books . . . to be kept. . . .
(d)	providing for the making of returns. . . .
(e)	exempting any person or class of persons from the operation of . . . this act;
(f)	requiring that all gasoline stored or offered for sale in Ontario shall be graded according to such scale as the regulations may prescribe;
(g)	requiring importers, manufacturers, jobbers and vendors of gasoline to indicate the grade and price of gasoline offered for sale;
(h)	fixing the grade or quality of gasoline which may be offered for sale;
(i)	providing for the sealing of pumps, tanks, reservoirs and other containers of gasoline;
(j)	prescribing the construction, equipment and operation of conveyances and containers used for the transportation and storage of gasoline, kerosene and distillate;
(jj) prescribing the method, manner and equipment to be used in the handling, storing, selling and disposing of gasoline, kerosene and distillate.
(k)	providing for the holding of inquiries into the operation of this act. . . .
(l)	generally for the better carrying out of the i>rovisions of this act.
That indicates the amount of control which is now being exercised by a province. To have the dominion duplicate the control is an unnecessary expense, one which is serving no good purpose.

Yes;
good gasoline is all very well; we are supposed to get good gasoline now, but there is grade 1 and grade 2, and both grades are different from what we used to get. For the gasoline we are using at present you have the carburettor set at one town; then you go to another town and have to set the carburettor again. Of course at present we are so short of gasoline that there is little automobile travel, but even if you drive around town, the carburettor has to be adjusted all the time.
. Mr. GIBSON: These measures, of course, have been brought in under the War Measures Act as a war necessity. I think the provinces would try to set their standards after the war, when there is no war necessity, to have similar standards for the whole country.
